      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Principal and\nroles\nA principal is\nOpen Catalog's\nidentity for a\nprogram, such as\nSnowflake, a Spark\njob, or a Kafka\nsink connector.\nOpen Catalog\ncreates and\nmanages it. When\nyou create a\nservice connection\nfor an engine,\nOpen Catalog\ncreates a service\nprincipal with a\nclient ID and a\nsecret. The engine\nsigns in with them\nthrough OAuth\n(open\nauthorization)\nclient\ncredentials, a\nstandard sign-in\nmethod for\nprograms, and gets\na short-lived\ntoken.\n\nPermissions reach\na principal\nthrough a chain.\nPrivileges go to a\ncatalog role, a\ncatalog role goes\nto a principal\nrole, and a\nprincipal role\ngoes to\nprincipals. A\ncatalog role\nbelongs to one\ncatalog, and a\nprincipal role\nspans the whole\naccount. When a\nclient asks for a\ntoken, it names\nthe principal role\nit wants, such as \nPRINCIPAL_ROLE:rea\nders, or\nPRINCIPAL_ROLE:ALL\nfor every role it\nholds.\n\nFor example, a\nKafka sink\nconnector signs in\nas principal\nkafka-sink, which\nholds principal\nrole sink-writer.\nIn catalog events,\ncatalog role\nevents-writer\nholds CATALOG_MANA\nGE_CONTENT and is\ngranted to\nsink-writer.\nSnowflake signs in\nas principal\nsnowflake-reader,\nwhose principal\nrole holds catalog\nrole events-reader\nwith\nTABLE_READ_DATA.\nSo the sink can\ncreate tables and\ncommit to them,\nand Snowflake can\nonly read them.\n\nTABLE_READ_DATA\nreads a table\nthrough\nshort-lived\nread-only storage\ncredentials.\nTABLE_CREATE and\nTABLE_WRITE_DATA\ncreate and write\ntables, and CATALO\nG_MANAGE_CONTENT\nbundles them with\nthe namespace and\nmetadata\nprivileges. The\n[Apache Polaris\naccess control gui\nde](https://polari\ns.apache.org/in-de\nv/unreleased/manag\ning-security/acces\ns-control/)\ndescribes the\nmodel.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-5a4c141e1bb3",
      "originalText": "Principal and roles\nA principal is Open Catalog's identity for a program, such as Snowflake, a Spark job, or a Kafka sink connector. Open Catalog creates and manages it. When you create a service connection for an engine, Open Catalog creates a service principal with a client ID and a secret. The engine signs in with them through OAuth (open authorization) client credentials, a standard sign-in method for programs, and gets a short-lived token.\n\nPermissions reach a principal through a chain. Privileges go to a catalog role, a catalog role goes to a principal role, and a principal role goes to principals. A catalog role belongs to one catalog, and a principal role spans the whole account. When a client asks for a token, it names the principal role it wants, such as PRINCIPAL_ROLE:readers, or PRINCIPAL_ROLE:ALL for every role it holds.\n\nFor example, a Kafka sink connector signs in as principal kafka-sink, which holds principal role sink-writer. In catalog events, catalog role events-writer holds CATALOG_MANAGE_CONTENT and is granted to sink-writer. Snowflake signs in as principal snowflake-reader, whose principal role holds catalog role events-reader with TABLE_READ_DATA. So the sink can create tables and commit to them, and Snowflake can only read them.\n\nTABLE_READ_DATA reads a table through short-lived read-only storage credentials. TABLE_CREATE and TABLE_WRITE_DATA create and write tables, and CATALOG_MANAGE_CONTENT bundles them with the namespace and metadata privileges. The [Apache Polaris access control guide](https://polaris.apache.org/in-dev/unreleased/managing-security/access-control/) describes the model.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "principal-and-roles"
      }
    },
