      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 8823934347,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-1e7b0087054d",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1910.8000000000002,
      "y": 1505.5,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 117.60000000000002,
      "height": 1015.0,
      "seed": 232565,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Under the hood\nEach catalog\nhas storage\nsettings: a\ndefault base\nlocation, such\nas s3://bucket\n/prefix,\noptional extra\nlocations, and\nan IAM (AWS\nIdentity and\nAccess\nManagement)\nrole in your\ncloud account.\nOpen Catalog\nhas one IAM\nuser for the\nwhole account.\nYou add that\nuser to the\nrole's trust\npolicy, with\nan external\nID, and Open\nCatalog\nassumes the\nrole to reach\ntable data,\nmetadata\nfiles, and\nmanifest\nfiles.\n\nWith that\naccess, Open\nCatalog writes\nthe new\nmetadata file\non each\ncommit. It\nprobably also\nuses the role\nto create the\nshort-lived\ncredentials\nthat it vends.\nThe [catalog\nsetup guide](h\nttps://docs.sn\nowflake.com/en\n/user-guide/op\nencatalog/crea\nte-catalog)\nlists the\nstorage\nsettings.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-1e7b0087054d",
      "originalText": "Under the hood\nEach catalog has storage settings: a default base location, such as s3://bucket/prefix, optional extra locations, and an IAM (AWS Identity and Access Management) role in your cloud account. Open Catalog has one IAM user for the whole account. You add that user to the role's trust policy, with an external ID, and Open Catalog assumes the role to reach table data, metadata files, and manifest files.\n\nWith that access, Open Catalog writes the new metadata file on each commit. It probably also uses the role to create the short-lived credentials that it vends. The [catalog setup guide](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/opencatalog/create-catalog) lists the storage settings.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "open-catalog-under-the-hood"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
